ay T1, Play T9, Play T6.\nThe game history of the previous player is: Play F2, Play F3, Play J1, Peng B4, Play W7, Peng W1, Play B3, Play B5, Play W6, Peng F4, Play B9, Play T4, Play W8, Play F1, Play B5, Play B5, Play T2, Play T6, Play F2, Play W5, Play B2, Play T5.\nThe followings are your valid actions:[Play T2; Play T3; Play B6; AnGang B6; Play J2; Play T1; Play B7; Play B8; Play T8]\u3002", "reasoning": "", "output": "Evaluation: \nLet me analyze my current hand and potential winning patterns.\n\n**Current State:**\n- Hand tiles: T1, T2, T3, T8, B6\u00d74, B7, B8, J2 (13 tiles)\n- Exposed meld: [T7, T7, T7, T7] (concealed kong)\n- I need 1 more tile to win (14 tiles total)\n\n**Potential Patterns:**\n1. **B6\u00d74 as concealed kong**: I have four B6 tiles, which can form a concealed kong for bonus points\n2. **T1-T2-T3**: Forms a chow sequence in Bamboo suit\n3. **B6-B7-B8**: Can form a chow sequence in Dots suit\n4. **Remaining tiles**: T8, J2 need to fit into the pattern\n\n**Promising Moves:**\n\n1. **AnGang B6** (Concealed Kong): This would convert B6\u00d74 into a meld, then draw a replacement tile. This gives:\n   - Patterns: Concealed Kong (2 pts), possibly Two Concealed Kongs (6 pts)\n   - After kong: Remaining hand would be T1, T2, T3, T8, B7, B8, J2 + new tile\n   - Creates flexibility for completing [T1-T2-T3], [B7-B8-B9 or B6-B7-B8]\n\n2. **Play T8**: Discard T8 to maintain [T1-T2-T3] chow and wait for completing B6-B7-B8 sequence (need B5 or B9) or forming pairs\n   \n3. **Play J2**: Keep the suited tiles for better combination potential\n\n**Analysis of AnGang B6:**\n- After forming kong, I'd have: [T7\u00d74 kong], [B6\u00d74 kong], and remaining tiles T1, T2, T3, T8, B7, B8, J2\n- Need to form 1 more meld + 1 pair from these 7 tiles + the replacement tile\n- [T1-T2-T3] already forms a chow\n- If I draw B5 or B9: can form [B7-B8-B9] or [B5-B6-B7] \n- Two Concealed Kongs (6 pts) + Concealed Hand potential + Out With Replacement Tile (8 pts) = significant scoring\n\nReason: \nMaking the concealed kong with B6\u00d74 is the strongest play because:\n1. It secures 2 points for Concealed Kong and potentially 6 points for Two Concealed Kongs\n2. The replacement tile draw gives an additional chance to complete the hand immediately (Out With Replacement Tile = 8 pts)\n3. My hand structure [T1-T2-T3] is already solid, and B7-B8 can flexibly combine with B5, B6, or B9\n4. The unshown tiles show B6 has 4 remaining (all in my hand), B7 has 3, B8 has 3, giving good odds\n5. This maximizes point potential while maintaining winning flexibility\n\nAnswer: AnGang B6"}
